# Copy to `.env` and adjust if needed. The defaults match docker-compose.yml.
#
# The one required var to run silo. Points at the Postgres from `pnpm db:up`.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://silo:silo@localhost:5432/silo

# Optional: a separate connection string for the pg-boss job queue pool.
# Falls back to DATABASE_URL when unset — leave commented for local dev.
# WORKER_DATABASE_URL=postgres://silo:silo@localhost:5432/silo

# Optional: the port @silo/api's Hono server listens on (pnpm --filter
# @silo/api dev/start). Defaults to 8787 when unset. The API is served at
# http://localhost:PORT (e.g. http://localhost:8787/health).
# PORT=8787

# Optional: the address @silo/api binds to. Defaults to loopback (127.0.0.1) —
# the API has NO auth, so binding wider exposes the whole store to your network.
# Set this only on a trusted, isolated network; it prints a loud warning.
# HOST=0.0.0.0

# Optional (but REQUIRED to use POST /api/ingest): the bearer token that
# gates BOTH:
#   1. the trusted ingest seam a local ingest tool (e.g. the future
#      `silo ingest x` CLI command) uses to capture a link WITH pre-extracted
#      sourceData (e.g. a Field Theory twitter bookmark) — POST /api/ingest is
#      closed by default: with this unset, EVERY request to /api/ingest gets
#      401, even from localhost.
#   2. OPTIONALLY, every other /api/* route (GET /health is exempt) — WHEN
#      this is set, every other route also requires a matching
#      `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header (401 without it). WHEN unset,
#      every other route has NO auth at all (today's default localhost
#      behavior — see docs/rules/api-hono.md's "Auth (there is none)"), only
#      /api/ingest is gated.
# Set this to widen protection to the whole API (e.g. deploying somewhere
# reachable off-host — see HOST above). A prod deployment that sets this MUST
# also make its web UI send the same token (or serve the UI from a trusted
# origin that injects it) or the UI's own /api/* calls will start 401ing —
# that web-UI auth story is not solved by this var alone.
# Pick a long random value (e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32`) — it is a real
# credential, keep it out of source control and shell history.
# SILO_API_TOKEN=

# Optional: expose the MCP server over HTTP (in addition to stdio) on this
# port, from the turnkey `@silo/app` process. UNSET -> MCP is stdio-only
# (today's default: a subprocess an MCP client launches, no network surface).
# SET -> the turnkey ALSO listens for MCP over HTTP at
# http://127.0.0.1:PORT/mcp, so a networked agent connects by inserting the
# API key. This listener is ALWAYS-CLOSED: it requires SILO_API_TOKEN (above)
# and every request must send `Authorization: Bearer <SILO_API_TOKEN>`. If
# you set this port but leave SILO_API_TOKEN unset, the process REFUSES to
# open the listener (loud stderr warning) rather than expose an
# unauthenticated networked MCP endpoint — stdio MCP still works. Binds
# loopback (127.0.0.1) only.
# SILO_MCP_HTTP_PORT=8788

# Optional: comma-separated list of origins allowed to call /api/* cross-
# origin (CORS). UNSET -> safe localhost defaults (http://localhost:5173 —
# the web UI's Vite dev server — and http://localhost:8787 — this API's own
# default port). SET -> exactly the listed origins, nothing implicit added.
# A production deployment should list every trusted origin explicitly, e.g.
# the packed Chrome extension's id and the deployed web UI's real origin:
#   SILO_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=chrome-extension://<extension-id>,https://silo.example.com
# NEVER set this to "*" — the allowlist IS the security boundary for this
# API (which otherwise has no auth by default); a wildcard would let any
# web page read/write/delete the whole store via the visitor's browser.
# SILO_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# Optional: the worker's scheduled maintenance jobs (packages/worker/src/jobs).
# The trash-purge cycle is also settable per-user in the web Settings screen;
# this env var is the worker-side default/override — an unset or malformed
# value falls back to the documented default below.
#
# Trash retention window in days for the daily `purge-trash` job. Defaults to 30.
# SILO_TRASH_PURGE_DAYS=30
#
# Minutes a link may sit at capture_status='enriching' before the ~5-minute
# `sweep-enriching` job considers it stranded and re-enqueues it. Defaults to 15.
# SILO_ENRICHING_STALE_MINUTES=15
